Character set tables (internal/charset/tables.go)

  windows-1252, bytes 0x80 to 0x9F: from the WHATWG Encoding Standard,
  index-windows-1252 (https://encoding.spec.whatwg.org/index-windows-1252.txt),
  which agrees with the Unicode Consortium's mapping
  MAPPINGS/VENDORS/MICSFT/WINDOWS/CP1252.TXT at every byte that mapping
  defines. The five bytes the Unicode mapping leaves undefined are decoded
  as U+FFFD. The 32 entries were written out from the specification by hand,
  without network access. internal/charset/charset_test.go checks them
  against the code points of the specification and against the copied
  windows-1252 table below, which was compared byte by byte with Python's
  codec; they have not been compared directly with the published index
  file.

  ISO/IEC 8859-15, the eight bytes where it differs from ISO-8859-1: from
  the Unicode Consortium's mapping MAPPINGS/ISO8859/8859-15.TXT, written
  out in the same way.

  The WHATWG Encoding Standard is licensed under the Creative Commons
  Attribution 4.0 International License. The Unicode mapping files are
  provided under the Unicode terms of use, which permit their use in
  products supporting the Unicode Standard.

Code page tables (internal/charset/codepages.go)

  Copied unchanged from go-fast-rtf (github.com/AOShei/go-fast-rtf,
  internal/codepage/tables.go), by the same author and under the same
  MIT License. Its provenance, as go-fast-rtf's NOTICE gives it:

  The tables of the single-byte code pages are the content of the mapping
  files published by the Unicode Consortium, written out as Go arrays:

    Windows 874, 1250, 1251, 1252, 1253, 1254, 1255, 1256, 1257, 1258:
      https://www.unicode.org/Public/MAPPINGS/VENDORS/MICSFT/WINDOWS/CP874.TXT
      https://www.unicode.org/Public/MAPPINGS/VENDORS/MICSFT/WINDOWS/CP1250.TXT
      ... through CP1258.TXT
    Mac OS Roman (code page 10000):
      https://www.unicode.org/Public/MAPPINGS/VENDORS/APPLE/ROMAN.TXT

  The files are provided by the Unicode Consortium and the vendors named
  in them (Microsoft Corporation, Apple Inc.) under the Unicode terms of
  use, which permit use of the mapping data. Bytes the files leave
  undefined are undefined in the tables (decoded as U+FFFD with a
  warning).

  The tables were transcribed while go-fast-rtf was written and have
  since been compared byte by byte with Python's codecs of the same code
  pages (which implement these mapping files), with no difference. Here,
  internal/charset/charset_test.go also checks the copied windows-1252
  table against the separately transcribed one above.

  ISO-8859-9 and ISO-8859-11 (TIS-620) are decoded with the windows-1254
  and windows-874 tables, as the WHATWG Encoding Standard maps their
  labels.

Double-byte code pages (internal/charset/dbcs.go, gen/gen_dbcs.py,
tables/cp932.txt, cp936.txt, cp949.txt, cp950.txt)

  Copied from go-fast-html (github.com/AOShei/go-fast-html,
  internal/charset, commit be5c679), by the same author and under the
  same MIT License: dbcs.go, with dbcsAt, userDefined (the user-defined
  areas of Shift_JIS and GBK, mapped to the Private Use Area as the WHATWG
  Encoding Standard's indexes do) and isLead from its charset.go, and the
  generator gen/gen_dbcs.py. go-fast-html copied them from go-fast-rtf
  (internal/codepage, commit b756076). As go-fast-html's NOTICE gives it:
  cp932.txt to cp950.txt are generated by gen/gen_dbcs.py from Python's
  codecs cp932, cp936, cp949 and cp950, which implement Microsoft's code
  pages as the Unicode Consortium publishes them (MAPPINGS/VENDORS/MICSFT/
  WINDOWS/CP932.TXT ... CP950.TXT). Mappings to C1 controls and the
  Private Use Area that the codecs add beyond the published files are
  left out. The first line of each names the Python version. The mapping
  data is used under the Unicode terms of use. The label lists follow the
  WHATWG Encoding Standard (CC BY 4.0).

GB18030, EUC-JP and ISO-2022-JP (internal/charset/cjk.go,
gen/gen_cjk_extra.py, tables/gb18030-2.txt, gb18030-4.txt, jis0208.txt,
jis0212.txt)

  The tables were generated on 2026-11-20 by
  internal/charset/gen/gen_cjk_extra.py from Python's codecs gb18030,
  cp932 (through the Shift_JIS to JIS X 0208 conversion) and euc_jp; the
  first line of each names the Python version. The mapping data is that
  of Python's codecs, distributed with Python under the Python Software
  Foundation License. gb18030-4.txt is laid out as the WHATWG Encoding
  Standard's index-gb18030-ranges. Two entries differ from the generated
  tables, in cjk.go: 0xA8BC is U+1E3F and four-byte pointer 7457 is
  U+E7C7, as the Encoding Standard has them (Python's codec has the two
  the other way round). The decoders follow the WHATWG Encoding Standard
  (CC BY 4.0), written from it as remembered.

